messages:
Alicia: Quick question — thinking about adding a small smoothing adapter at the ingress that: applies a tiny local token buffer, enforces a per-client burst cap, and emits periodic aggregate debt to the central quota. Would reduce hot-path reads to the quota service. Thoughts?

Samir: Sounds promising. Main worry is correctness for the fairness scheduler — local buffers could hide instantaneous demand and accidentally starve low-volume tenants if we don't surface enough info.

Nina: Could we make the adapter 'non-stealing' by tagging admitted tokens with an admission epoch so the fairness layer can account for them later? Central scheduler would still receive the same logical events, just delayed.

Matt: Tagging is doable but increases the proto surface and forces changes to eviction policies. Alternative: emit "shadow" credit events every N ms so the central scheduler sees near-real-time footprint without synchronous reads on every token.

Alicia: Shadow events sound like a nice middle ground. Perf wins vs telemetry cost tradeoff — we should bound the shadow rate and make it adaptive to client volume.

Samir: Also need clear backpressure semantics. If central denies a reserve, adapter must either block the request, return an explicit fallback, or flip to bypass mode (send directly to runtime). Bypass keeps latency but can violate fairness.

Nina: Proposal for interface shape: ReserveRequest { client_id, tokens, epoch_id, allow_fallback } as a simple JSON RPC. That keeps it flexible for Private deployments.

Matt: I'd push for a gRPC proto so we get streaming and stronger typing. Rough sketch:

```proto
service QuotaBridge {
  rpc Reserve(ReserveRequest) returns (ReserveResponse);
}
message ReserveRequest {
  string client_id = 1;
  int64 tokens = 2;
  string epoch_id = 3; // adapters tag this
  bool allow_fallback = 4;
  string trace_id = 5;
}
message ReserveResponse {
  bool granted = 1;
  int64 remaining = 2;
  enum Mode { STRICT = 0; BEST_EFFORT = 1; }
  Mode applied_mode = 3;
}
```

Alicia: Good. We should also define telemetry counters: adapter_token_sink, shadow_event_rate, fallback_count, reserve_latency_p95, fairness_delta_by_tenant.

infra-bot: deploy-bot: Canary window scheduled: eng-platform-canary-2026-03-22 03:00 UTC

Samir: Add a regression eval to the plan — run the fairness harness with a skewed workload before/after and measure headroom for small tenants.

Nina: One more operational note: for Private/on-prem installs, epoch_id should be signed (HMAC) to prevent client-side tampering if adapters run in semi-trusted network segments. That implies a small KMS integration.

Matt: Timeline idea: RFC + proto + adapter PoC in 2 weeks, canary + eval in 4 weeks, gradual rollout over 8 weeks if metrics look good.

Alicia: I'll draft the RFC and include the proto sketch and a migration plan (feature flag per region, canary on low-volume tenants). @Samir @Nina please review when I post the draft. :thumbsup:
